---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import MasonXMonogram from '../../components/MasonXMonogram.astro';
import TitleBlock from '../../components/TitleBlock.astro';

const title = "Half the Block, Two-Thirds the Price | TMM Blog";
const description = "A half block costs about two-thirds of a full one. Miss the count and your bid is short. Why halves are a premium unit you have to count, not estimate.";
const canonical = "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/half-block-cost/";

const blogPostingSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "Half the Block, Two-Thirds the Price",
  "description": description,
  "image": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/assets/og-image.png",
  "datePublished": "2026-06-22",
  "dateModified": "2026-06-22",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Ryan Shanley",
    "jobTitle": "Founder",
    "worksFor": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Powder River Masonry" },
    "url": canonical,
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "The Masonry Modeler",
    "url": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/",
  },
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Field Notes", "item": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Half the Block, Two-Thirds the Price", "item": canonical },
  ],
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(blogPostingSchema)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <MasonXMonogram />
  <TitleBlock sheet="B1.4 — Half the Block, Two-Thirds the Price" ctaLabel="Join Waitlist →" ctaHref="/#waitlist" />

  <div class="section-divider">
    <span>Field Notes · B1.4</span>
    <span class="scale-marks"></span>
    <span>The Money</span>
  </div>

  <section class="blog-post-hero">
    <div class="blog-post-hero-inner">
      <div class="post-kicker">Field Notes</div>
      <h1>
        Half the Block, Two-Thirds the
        <span class="accent">Price</span>
      </h1>
      <div class="post-meta">
        <span><strong>Ryan Shanley</strong></span>
        <span>June 2026</span>
        <span>4 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="blog-post-body">

    <p>
      Everybody treats a half block like half a block. Half the size, half the cost, round it
      off, move on. That assumption is costing you money on every bid, and most masonry
      estimating software is built to make the same mistake.
    </p>

    <p>
      A half doesn't cost half. It costs about two-thirds of a full unit.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sit with that for a second. A half does half the work of a full block — covers half the
      length — but you pay roughly two-thirds the price for it. That's a premium of about a
      third on every spot a half lands. It's not a discount unit. It's your most expensive
      block per inch of wall.
    </p>

    <h2>And you use more of them than you think</h2>

    <p>
      Halves aren't a corner case. They're everywhere a wall stops being a clean, full-length
      run. Every wall end. Every opening jamb. Every course that turns a corner. Every length
      that doesn't divide even on the block.
    </p>

    <p>
      On a real building that's not a handful — it's hundreds. Hundreds of premium units, each
      one carrying that third-over cost, every one of them a separate piece somebody has to
      order, stage, and lay.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the count matters in the one direction you can't fix later. If your takeoff is short on
      halves, your bid is short on money — and you don't find out until the order comes in heavier
      than the number you handed the GC.
    </p>

    <h2>A half is its own order</h2>

    <p>
      Here's the other half of the problem. A half isn't a full block you cut on site. It's its
      own unit — its own SKU on the order.
    </p>

    <p>
      If it's not on the takeoff as its own line, one of two things happens. You forget it, and
      now the crew's on the wall short a piece, waiting. Or you guess, and you're either paying
      for halves you didn't need or scrambling for halves you did. Neither one is estimating.
      That's gambling with a material that carries a premium.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        A half costs me near what a full costs and covers half the wall. I need to know exactly
        how many, not a guess off the square footage. Get that number wrong and the whole bid's
        wrong — and it's wrong on the side that comes out of my pocket.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>You can't get a half count from area</h2>

    <p>
      This is the part the math can't fake. Area math gives you square footage. Square footage
      gives you a rough block count and a waste factor. It will never give you a count of halves,
      because a half isn't an area — it's a specific unit that exists in a specific place.
    </p>

    <p>
      The only way to know how many halves a building needs is to lay the wall and see where they
      fall. Where the run ends. Where the opening cuts in. Where the corner turns. The halves come
      out of the coursing — they're a result of how the wall actually goes together, not a
      percentage you sprinkle on top of a square-foot number.
    </p>

    <p>
      A tool that hands you a real half count had to have laid the wall to get it. A tool that
      started from areas can't — there's nowhere in that model for a half to live. That's the
      whole argument for an <a href="/cmu-block-count/">exact CMU block count</a> over square
      footage times a factor: the breakdown is the number that matters, and area math never
      produces one.
    </p>

    <h2>What The Masonry Modeler does</h2>

    <p>
      The Masonry Modeler starts from the coursing, so the halves are already there. You draw the
      wall, it renders it the way it gets laid, and the halves fall where they fall — ends, jambs,
      corners, odd lengths.
    </p>

    <p>
      The takeoff lists them as their own line, with a real count, priced as the premium unit they
      are. Not a percentage. Not folded into the full-block number. A count you can order from and
      a number you can bid on.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you've been treating halves like half a block, your last few bids were probably short and
      you never knew it. I'd like to show you where that money was going.
    </p>

    <a href="/#waitlist" class="post-cta-inline">Join the waitlist →</a>

  </article>

  <div class="blog-post-footer">
    <a href="/blog/" class="blog-back-link">← All posts</a>
  </div>

</Layout>
